! Containers on the WSL2 host — the ruling and its tripwire

The lararium kit runs as a compose-shaped stack in containers behind the WSL2 instance ([[lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/setup]]). Two engines can carry it on a Windows 11 home PC today: Docker Engine installed inside the distro, and Microsoft's own WSL containers (`wslc`), generally available since 2026-09-29. This carrier rules between them and names the condition that reverses the ruling.

''Ruling (2026-10-05):'' rootful Docker Engine inside the distro carries the stack. `wslc` stays off the path until it runs a `compose.yaml` unchanged. Re-read this ruling at every `wsl --update`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/wslc>>

!! WSL containers (`wslc`) — what shipped

|! fact |! reading |! grade |
| name | "WSL containers"; Microsoft writes WSLC / WSLc; the CLI is `wslc.exe`, alias `container.exe`. No `wsl --container` verb exists. "WSL 3" names no product — 2.9.13 pre-release jumped to 3.0.1 to carry the GA | high |
| timeline | Build 2026 (06-02) announce → public preview in WSL 2.9.3 (06-29, pre-release line) → GA in WSL 3.0.1 (09-29). The 2.7.x line never carried it; `wsl --update` moves a host to 3.0.1 | high |
| runtime | a stock Moby stack — dockerd 25.0.3, containerd 2.2.4, runc 1.3.3 — in its own utility VM, separate from the distro VM, with its own `storage.vhdx` under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\wslc\sessions\`. Microsoft never names the engine; three independent teardowns agree | medium-high |
| CLI | Docker-shaped: `wslc run/build/exec`, `wslc image …`, `wslc container …`, `wslc network …`, `wslc system info`, `wslc events`; release notes say "for docker parity". Images, Containerfiles and registries work | high |
| Docker API | not exposed. No socket, no `DOCKER_HOST`. Compose, Testcontainers, Dev Containers from WSL paths, buildx and docker-in-docker cannot target it (issues #40976, #40957) | high |
| compose | absent at GA. GA post: "Our top feature request for WSLc is adding compose support, and this will be our focus for our next iterations" — aim: `wslc compose up` on an unchanged `compose.yaml`. Tracked at #40948; a `feature/compose` branch holds a proof of concept whose parser accepts only seven keys. No date | high on intent, low on timing |
| from inside the distro | `wslc` does not run from a WSL distro and `\\wsl.localhost` paths fail; every `-v` source resolves as a Windows path (#41105, #40957) | high |
| capabilities | `--cap-add`, `--cap-drop`, `--read-only`, `--privileged` absent "by design … due to some security concerns but it is being actively investigated" (#41545). No `--device`, no USB | high |
| Windows Home | no edition restriction in any Microsoft text; it rides Virtual Machine Platform like WSL 2, no Hyper-V role. The WSL lead: it "works anywhere WSL is supported today" | high by inference |
| `.wslconfig` | no new keys. Per-session CPU, memory and storage live in `%LOCALAPPDATA%\wslc\settings.yaml` and the SDK's `SessionSettings`. "Capabilities" in the release notes means CLI feature lists | medium-high |
| Docker Desktop | Microsoft: the third-party tools "will also all benefit from these lower level platform changes" — never "replace". Docker Inc. has published no response | high |
| cost on a home PC | a second utility VM (`vmmemwslc-cli`, default 2 vCPU / 2 GB, 32 GB dynamic VHDX) beside the WSL 2 VM; idle teardown landed in 2.9.x | medium |

The community discovered the session VM already runs stock Moby and that compose, Dev Containers and Testcontainers all work unmodified against its socket — an unofficial route Microsoft has neither blessed nor closed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/engine-in-distro>>

!! Docker Engine inside the distro — the path that carries the stack

Rootful Docker Engine with the compose plugin, supervised by systemd inside Ubuntu 24.04, runs a `compose.yaml` unchanged, exposes `docker.sock` to tooling, carries Apache-2.0 licensing and no Windows-side program, and runs on Windows Home.

''Two package sources; pick one, never both.''

|! source |! line |! reading |
| Ubuntu archive | `sudo apt install docker.io docker-compose-v2 docker-buildx` | one line, Canonical-maintained; 29.1.3 / compose 2.40.3 in noble-updates. ''The family default.'' |
| Docker's apt repo | `docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin` | newest engine and compose, Docker-supported on 22.04 / 24.04 / 26.04; needs the signing key and a sources file; conflicts with the archive packages, which must leave first |

''Rootless stays off the table.'' WSL 2.9 moved the cgroup v2 hierarchy under `/wsl-user/distro-N/…`, so rootless Docker and rootless Podman fail on `cgroup.controllers` (microsoft/WSL #41492, open since 2026-09-01; 3.0.1 carries no fix). Rootful dockerd reads unaffected. The `docker` group therefore stays root-equivalent on this host; the runbook says so rather than hiding it.

''WSL2 specifics worth knowing:'' cgroup v2 runs by default; dockerd 25+ handles nft-based iptables; `-p` ports reach Windows `localhost` by default, and other LAN devices need `networkingMode=mirrored` or a `netsh portproxy`; distros are per Windows account, so a household that shares the stack shares the account.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/fallbacks>>

!! Fallbacks, ranked

# ''Rancher Desktop'' (moby backend) — officially lists Windows 11 Home, Apache-2.0, gives the same `docker` and `docker compose` inside the Ubuntu distro through its WSL integration, adds a GUI for a non-technical household member. Runs its own `rancher-desktop` distro beside Ubuntu; k3s can stay off.
# ''Docker Desktop'' — its requirements page (read 2026-10-06) names "Windows 11 64-bit: Enterprise, Pro, or Education version 23H2 (build 22631) or higher" and WSL 2.1.5+ for the WSL 2 backend; Home appears only in the callout "Windows Home or Education editions only allow you to run Linux containers", which the page never reconciles with its own list. It installs and runs on Home in practice, and the licence (2026-08-26) grants a household free use; on paper unsupported, so no support recourse. Its WSL integration places a second `docker` inside the distro, and while Desktop runs it restarts the WSL VM on its own (which matters to a raw vhdx backup): one engine, never both.
# ''Podman'' — rootful works; rootless hits #41492; compose fidelity reads good but short of full. Podman Desktop's WSL provider runs on Home; its own machine distro is a second VM.

Not today: `wslc` (no compose, no socket); nerdctl + containerd (works, more hand assembly, no apt packaging).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/tripwire>>

!! The tripwire — what reverses the ruling

Any one of these moves `wslc` to the primary path and the in-distro engine to a fallback:

# `wslc compose up` runs an unchanged `compose.yaml` — watch microsoft/WSL #40948 and each release note under `wsl --update`.
# A Docker-API endpoint ships officially (#40976), so `docker compose` and tooling target the wslc session.
# `wslc` runs from inside the distro with `\\wsl.localhost` paths (#41105), which the lararium's bind mounts need.

Separately, a fix for #41492 reopens rootless Docker, which retires the root-equivalent `docker` group.

When the tripwire turns: `stand-linux.sh` step 5b reads for `wslc` first and reports the in-distro engine as the fallback; `witness.sh` gains a `wslc compose` row; this carrier re-trues its ruling line and date.

<<~/ahu>>

